1
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:28,380
to allow them to go to completion in a reasonable time period. If these reactions were heated

2
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:32,700
in a closed container, crushed airway fills up inside the container and it may explode.

3
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:38,460
If these reactions were heated in an open container, then the solvent may evaporate from the system.

4
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:45,100
The method of refluxing solves both of the above problems by allowing a chemist to heat

5
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:51,020
a reaction at its boiling point without any materials evaporating or any containers exploding.

6
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:57,420
Refluxing works by heating a solution to boiling and condensing the resulting vapor by continuous

7
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:03,420
cooling. Because approximately 80 percent of all organic reactions involve refluxing,

8
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:10,300
this technique is an important one to understand. To carry out a reflux, you will need a cork ring,

9
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:17,020
a round bottom flask, a stir plate in stir bar or boiling stones, a condenser, grease,

10
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:24,140
a ring stand, two extension clamps with fasteners, two pieces of thin walled rubber tubing,

11
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:31,580
three hose clips, a heating mantle, an iron ring, and a variac. The first step of refluxing involves

12
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:37,260
choosing an appropriate solvent. A good refluxing solvent will dissolve all of the reagents but not

13
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:42,620
react with them and it will boil at a temperature high enough to allow the reaction to proceed

14
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:48,300
rapidly. After choosing your solvent, dissolve all of the reagents in it and transfer the solution

15
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:54,620
to a round bottom flask. The round bottom flask should be no more than half full. Then add either

16
00:01:54,620 --> 00:02:00,700
a stir bar or boiling stones to the solution to avoid bumping. Bumping is the eruption of large

17
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:06,620
bubbles from a solution due to super heating which results in material loss or even a fire.

18
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:11,420
Magnetic stirrers disallow bumping because they create turbulence in the solution

19
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:17,100
which disrupt the large bubbles. Boiling stones are also able to disrupt the large bubbles

20
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:21,900
because these porous materials produce a steady stream of fine air bubbles when heated in a

21
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:28,300
solvent. Always place boiling stones in the solution before heating. If boiling stones

22
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:33,500
are added to a hot solution, the liquid may instantaneously boil and shoot out of the

23
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:40,060
flask. To assemble the reflux apparatus, clamp the round bottom flask containing your sample to

24
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:45,740
a ring stand. Lightly grease the male end of the condenser and attach it to the round bottom flask.

25
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:51,420
Attach the water inlet tubing to the bottom connection and the water outlet tubing to the

26
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:56,700
top connection. Secure the tubing with hose clips to prevent the tubes from popping off

27
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:02,060
and flooding the lab due to pressure changes in the water. Secure an appropriately sized

28
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:07,580
heating mantle and iron ring below the flask. If you do not have an appropriately sized heating

29
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:13,340
mantle, then use one that is too big. Never use a heating mantle that is too small because heat is

30
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:18,540
not easily transferred between the mantle and glass due to port contact, which causes the mantle to

31
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:23,900
burn out. If the mantle is too big for the flask, then add sand to fill in the spaces.

32
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:31,180
Finally, plug the heating mantle into a variable voltage transformer or variac. Never directly

33
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:37,020
into the wall. Before starting the reflux, make sure that the apparatus is open at the top of

34
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:42,620
the condenser. Never heat a closed system because the pressure buildup may cause an explosion.

35
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:48,140
To reflux a reaction, double check that all of the joints are secured with hose clips and

36
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:54,220
start the water flow. Slowly increase the setting on the variac until the solution begins a gentle

37
00:03:54,220 --> 00:04:00,700
boil. You will see a ring of vapor or condensate form above the solution. This vapor enters the

38
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:07,340
condenser, cools, and the resulting liquid drops back into the flask. Carefully adjust the amount

39
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:12,540
of heat that is delivered to the solution so that the ring of condensate stabilizes about halfway

40
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:17,420
up the condenser. If the ring is too high, sample may be lost out of the condenser.

41
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,700
If the ring is too low, then the reaction will occur over too long of a time period.

42
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:28,300
After the reflux is finished, remove the heat, let the round bottom flask cool,

43
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:34,060
and shut off the water. During your laboratory experiences, you may need to run your reflux under

44
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:40,700
dry conditions. To accomplish this, simply place a clean dry drying tube at the top of the condenser.

45
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:45,580
This drying tube allows the system to be open while preventing the atmosphere from reaching

46
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:51,340
your solution. Alternatively, insert a T connector on the top of the condenser

47
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:55,980
and connect the other two arms of the connector to a nitrogen source and a bubbler.

48
00:04:56,060 --> 00:05:00,540
You may also wish to add reagents to your solution while the reflux is occurring.

49
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:06,700
To accomplish this addition, you will need an addition funnel, which is a separatory funnel

50
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:11,740
with a ground glass joint, a glass stopper that fits the funnel, and a Claisen adapter.

51
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:16,380
Simply fit the Claisen adapter on top of the round bottom flask,

52
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:20,460
attach the condenser to the joint directly above the round bottom flask,

53
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:24,780
and attach the addition funnel to the side arm. Remember to grease all the joints.

54
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:31,180
Alternatively, you may use a three-necked flask in place of the single-necked flask in Claisen

55
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:36,060
adapter. Just remember to place a rubber septum on the remaining neck of the flask.

56
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:40,140
If your addition funnel does not have a pressure-equalizing arm,

57
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:43,740
then you must periodically remove the glass stopper to break the vacuum.

58
00:05:45,660 --> 00:05:50,140
Refluxing allows a canvas to heat a reaction over an extended time period

59
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:53,900
without worrying about exploding glassware or solving an aberration.
